# Line widths: scale_linewidth

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`scale_linewidth()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/scale_linewidth.html), which covers
`scale_linewidth_binned()` too, in the order the page gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's
code first and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names
what it is waiting for, so the page is also a list of what remains.

`scale_linewidth()` maps a continuous column to the width of lines and of polygons' outlines,
between a thinnest and a thickest width; `scale_linewidth_binned()` cuts the column into bins first.
The `size` aesthetic can still set a line's width, for historical reasons, but `linewidth` keeps the
width of a stroke apart from the size of an area. Giggly has none of this yet: it draws paths and
lines as wide as a layer sets them, but maps no `linewidth`, and it has no scale functions.

## Line width from a column

```r
p <- ggplot(economics, aes(date, unemploy, linewidth = uempmed)) +
  geom_line(lineend = "round")
p
p + scale_linewidth("Duration of\nunemployment")
p + scale_linewidth(range = c(0, 4))
```

A scale's first argument titles its legend, and `range` sets the thinnest and the thickest
widths.

Not in Giggly yet: mapping `linewidth`, `scale_linewidth()` and its `range` option, the legend that
reads it back, and titles and labels.

## Binned widths

```r
# Binning can sometimes make it easier to match the scaled data to the legend
p + scale_linewidth_binned()
```

Not in Giggly yet: mapping `linewidth`, `scale_linewidth_binned()` and the legend that reads it
back.
